Here is your complete Ubuntu 18.04 + ROS Melodic + Livox Mid-360S + u-blox 7 recording procedure, starting from connecting the GPS and LiDAR.

We'll save two separate datasets:

Raw GPS NMEA data (.txt)
Livox LiDAR + IMU data (.bag)
PART 1 — GPS SETUP AND RECORDING
Step 1. Connect the u-blox 7 GPS

Connect your GPS receiver via USB.

Check its serial port:

bash
ls /dev/ttyACM*


Your device should appear as:

text
/dev/ttyACM0

Step 2. Start GPSD

Open Terminal 1:

bash
sudo systemctl stop gpsd
sudo systemctl stop gpsd.socket


Start GPSD:

bash
sudo gpsd -N -n /dev/ttyACM0


Keep this terminal open.

Step 3. Start raw GPS recording

Open Terminal 2:

bash
mkdir -p ~/gps_raw


Start recording:

bash
gpspipe -r | tee ~/gps_raw/ublox7_$(date +%Y%m%d_%H%M%S).txt


You'll see raw NMEA sentences:

text
$GPGGA,...
$GPRMC,...
$GPGSV,...
$GPGSA,...


Your data is simultaneously being saved to a text file.

Step 4. Stop GPS recording

Press:

text
Ctrl+C

Step 5. Verify GPS recording
bash
ls -lh ~/gps_raw/


Count recorded lines:

bash
wc -l ~/gps_raw/*.txt


View the data:

bash
tail -20 ~/gps_raw/*.txt

PART 2 — LIVox MID-360S SETUP

Your known configuration:

Parameter	Value
LiDAR IP	192.168.1.193
Laptop Ethernet IP	192.168.1.50
Ethernet interface	enp0s31f6
LiDAR model	MID-360S
Step 1. Connect the LiDAR Ethernet cable

Connect your MID-360S to the laptop through Ethernet.

Step 2. Configure laptop IP

Open Terminal 3:

bash
sudo ip link set enp0s31f6 up


Assign the IP:

bash
sudo ip addr flush dev enp0s31f6
sudo ip addr add 192.168.1.50/24 dev enp0s31f6


Verify:

bash
ip -br addr


You should see:

text
enp0s31f6 UP 192.168.1.50/24

Step 3. Verify LiDAR connection
bash
ping -c 4 192.168.1.193


You should receive four replies.

Step 4. Source ROS and your Livox workspace
bash
source /opt/ros/melodic/setup.bash
source ~/ws_livox/devel/setup.bash

Step 5. Start Livox MID-360S driver
bash
roslaunch livox_ros_driver2 rviz_MID360s.launch


Keep this terminal open.

PART 3 — VERIFY LIDAR AND IMU

Open Terminal 4:

bash
source /opt/ros/melodic/setup.bash
source ~/ws_livox/devel/setup.bash


Check available topics:

bash
rostopic list


Expected:

text
/livox/lidar
/livox/imu


Check LiDAR frequency:

bash
rostopic hz /livox/lidar


Check IMU frequency:

bash
rostopic hz /livox/imu


Both should show incoming messages.

Inspect IMU:

bash
rostopic echo -n 1 /livox/imu


Inspect LiDAR:

bash
rostopic echo -n 1 /livox/lidar

PART 4 — RECORD LIDAR + IMU ROS BAG

Open Terminal 5:

bash
source /opt/ros/melodic/setup.bash
source ~/ws_livox/devel/setup.bash


Create the recording directory:

bash
mkdir -p ~/livox_bags


Start recording:

bash
rosbag record -O ~/livox_bags/mid360s_$(date +%Y%m%d_%H%M%S).bag /livox/lidar /livox/imu


Let it record for however long you need.

To stop:

text
Ctrl+C

PART 5 — VERIFY YOUR ROS BAG

List recordings:

bash
ls -lh ~/livox_bags/


Inspect the latest recording:

bash
rosbag info ~/livox_bags/NAME_OF_YOUR_FILE.bag


You should see something similar to:

text
topics:
    /livox/lidar    XXXX msgs : sensor_msgs/PointCloud2
    /livox/imu      XXXX msgs : sensor_msgs/Imu


Check that:

Both topics are present.
Both message counts are nonzero.
Duration matches your recording time.
File size is reasonable.
